# Nicknames of Indian Cities: The Poetic Names on the Map

> Kings, traders, and colour-washed walls gave India's cities their
> second names. Examiners adore them precisely because they are pure
> association — no reasoning, just recall. The Pink City, the City of
> Joy, the Manchester of India: each nickname encodes something real
> about a city's history, and knowing the *why* makes the *what*
> impossible to forget.

## 1. The Colour Cities of Rajasthan

| City | Nickname | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Jaipur | Pink City | Walls painted terracotta pink for the 1876 visit of the Prince of Wales; kept ever since |
| Jodhpur | Blue City | Indigo-washed houses around Mehrangarh, originally Brahmin homes |
| Jodhpur | Sun City | The sun shines nearly every day of the year |
| Udaipur | White City / City of Lakes / Venice of the East | White marble palaces; the lake palace network of Pichola |
| Bikaner | Camel Country | Camel breeding farms and the camel festival |

Jaipur's pink is not decorative whimsy: in 1876 Maharaja Sawai Ram
Singh had the whole old city painted pink — a colour of hospitality —
for the visit of Albert Edward, Prince of Wales, and a later law made
repainting in pink compulsory for the walled city. Jodhper's blue is
variously attributed to Brahmin caste marking and to the colour's
supposed mosquito-repelling and heat-reflecting properties.

## 2. The Industrial and Commercial Metaphors

[definition]
The **"Manchester" nicknames** honour Manchester, England — the
original textile city of the industrial revolution. Indian cities
that replicated its mills in a particular industry inherited the
title, usually with a regional qualifier.
[/definition]

| City | Nickname | Industry behind it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ahmedabad | Manchester of India | Textile mills of the 19th–20th century |
| Coimbatore | Manchester of South India | Cotton textiles |
| Kanpur | Manchester of the East (leather city) | Leather and textile mills |
| Jamshedpur | Steel City of India / Pittsburgh of India | Tata Steel, India's first steel plant (1907) |
| Bhilai | Steel City (of MP/Chhattisgarh) | Bhilai Steel Plant, Soviet collaboration |
| Chennai | Detroit of India | Automobile manufacturing hub |
| Bengaluru | Silicon Valley of India | IT industry |
| Hyderabad | Cyberabad / Genome Valley | IT and biotechnology |
| Mumbai | City of Dreams / Financial Capital | Bollywood and the RBI/BSE |
| Kochi | Queen of the Arabian Sea | Historic spice port |
| Kolkata | City of Joy | From Dominique Lapierre's 1985 book of that name |
| Surat | Diamond City / City of Bridges | 90% of the world's diamonds are cut and polished here |
| Panipat | City of Weavers | Handloom re-cycling of wool, historic battles aside |

## 3. Cities of Temples, Nawabs, and Learning

| City | Nickname |
| --- | --- |
| Varanasi | Spiritual capital of India / City of Ghats |
| Bhubaneswar | Temple City of India |
| Ayodhya, Mathura | Birthplace cities (Rama and Krishna respectively) |
| Lucknow | City of Nawabs / Constantinople of India |
| Allahabad (Prayagraj) | Sangam City / King of Pilgrimages |
| Haridwar | Gateway to God (Har ki Pauri) |
| Rishikesh | Yoga Capital of the World |
| Amritsar | Golden City (Golden Temple) |
| Ujjain | City of Mahakal |
| Madurai | Athens of the East (temple city of Meenakshi) |
| Tirupati | Spiritual capital of Andhra |
| Nashik | Wine Capital of India / Kalpeshwar of the west |
| Pune | Oxford of the East |
| Kashi (Varanasi) | City of Light |

The Pune–Madurai pair both claim "Athens/Oxford of the East" style
titles for their universities — Pune for its modern educational
density, Madurai for the ancient Tamil sangam tradition. Papers
usually tie "Oxford of the East" to Pune and "Athens of the East" to
Madurai, though older books occasionally swap them; check the
options for the city, not the empire.

## 4. Nature's Nicknames and the Geography Set

| City | Nickname | Geographical reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mysuru (Mysore) | City of Palaces / Sandalwood City | Amba Vilas and sandalwood craft |
| Alappuzha (Alleppey) | Venice of the East (Kerala's claim) | Backwater canals and houseboats |
| Shillong | Scotland of the East | Rolling hills and colonial-era comparison |
| Dibrugarh | Tea City of India | Assam tea gardens |
| Guwahati | Gateway to North-East India | Entry corridor to the region |
| Visakhapatnam | City of Destiny / Goa of the East | Beaches and harbour |
| Nainital | Lake District of India | The tal (lake) ring |
| Udaipur | (again) City of Lakes | The most common "City of Lakes" answer |

Two nicknames are genuinely contested, and good papers exploit that:
**City of Lakes** is claimed by both Udaipur (the royal lake system)
and Bhopal (the upper and lower lakes); most exams accept either but
frame the options to allow only one — pick by elimination. **Venice
of the East** similarly splits between Udaipur and Alappuzha, and the
safe rule is "Venice of the East = Alappuzha" in southern papers and
"= Udaipur" in northern ones; prefer the option pairing that matches
the rest of the question.

[example]
A question asks: "Which Indian city is known as the Pink City?" with
Jodhpur as the trap. Jodhpur is the **Blue** City (and Sun City);
Jaipur is the Pink City — the 1876 royal-visit repaint. A follow-up
trap: "City of Joy?" — the tempting wrong answer is Varanasi
(spiritual); the answer is Kolkata, from Lapierre's novel. Attach the
story (pink for a prince, joy from a book) and the pairs stop
colliding.
[/example]

[summary]
City nicknames cluster memorably: Rajasthan gives Pink (Jaipur), Blue
and Sun (Jodhpur), White and Lakes (Udaipur). Industry gives
Manchester of India (Ahmedabad), Manchester of South India
(Coimbatore), Steel City (Jamshedpur), Detroit of India (Chennai) and
Silicon Valley of India (Bengaluru). Poetry gives City of Joy
(Kolkata, after Lapierre), City of Dreams (Mumbai), Queen of the
Arabian Sea (Kochi) and Scotland of the East (Shillong). Learning
gives Oxford of the East (Pune) and Athens of the East (Madurai).
Contested titles — City of Lakes, Venice of the East — should be
answered by elimination against the other options.
[/summary]
